<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Balikh</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Balikh River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Balikh is a river of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> that rises near the Turkish–Syrian border and flows south for about 100 kilometres to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raqqa">Raqqa</a>. Its valley was a corridor for trade and armies, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a> near its upper course and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> city of Tuttul, at Tell Bi'a, at its mouth. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it formed part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyar-mudar">Diyar Mudar</a>, the western province of the Jazira.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/balikh-river.jpg" alt="Balikh" width="320" height="343" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: X. Guimard · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bassin_Tigre_Euphrate.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Balikh is a perennial river that rises in the karstic spring of Ain al-Arous, just south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> border, and flows due south to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raqqa">Raqqa</a>. It is the second largest tributary of the Euphrates in Syria, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabur-river">Khabur River</a>.</p><p>Besides the spring it receives water from periodic streams and wadis draining the Harran Plain to the north and the plains on either side of its valley, among them the Jullab, the Wadi Qaramogh and the Wadi al-Kheder. These wadis can carry large amounts of water after heavy rain. Numerous dried-up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> in the old city of Harran suggest that the water table was once much higher.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The river forms the heart of a rich cultural region. Settlement mounds on both banks go back in some cases to the Late Neolithic, in the 6th millennium BCE. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>, in the 3rd millennium BCE, ancient Tuttul, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raqqa">Raqqa</a> at the mouth of the Balikh, and Tell Chuera to the north were important cities. Over the millennia nomadic tribes and settled populations interacted, and sometimes one gained the upper hand over the other.</p><p>In classical antiquity the region was called Osrhoene, with its capital at Edessa. Edessa, known in Arabic as ar-Ruha', and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a>, the Roman Carrhae, appear in Muslim and Jewish traditions respectively in the stories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a> and the other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> patriarchs. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-muslim-conquests">Islamic conquest</a> in the 7th century CE the region took the name of an Arab tribe, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyar-mudar">Diyar Mudar</a>, the land of the Mudar. In 762 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Mansur built the garrison city of ar-Rafiqa where the Balikh meets the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, and it merged with the Hellenistic city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kallinikos">Kallinikos</a> into the urban agglomeration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raqqa">Raqqa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Nineteenth-century European travellers noted remains in the valley, but the first investigations came in 1938, when Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/max-mallowan">Max Mallowan</a> spent six weeks at five sites dating from the seventh to the second millennium BCE. In 1969 a French team under Jacques Cauvin exposed eight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> occupation levels at one site.</p><p>Excavations at Tell Hammam al-Turkman began under Maurits N. van <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borgloon">Loon</a> in 1981 and continued under Diederik J. W. Meijer from 1988 to 2001, providing a well-stratified sequence for the valley's settlement history. Tell Sabi Abyad, one of the oldest sites, has been excavated under Peter Akkermans. The Turkish site of Göbekli Tepe stands on a hill directly north of the Harran Plains that feed the Balikh system.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Balikh River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Banu Numayr were a Bedouin tribe of the Amir ibn Sa'sa'a who moved into the Diyar Mudar, the region between the Euphrates and the <b>Balikh</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/numayrids">Numayrids</a></li><li><blockquote>Tuttul stood at Tell Bi'a where the <b>Balikh</b> river meets the Euphrates, an ideal place for river and caravan traffic.</blockquote><a href="/state/tuttul">Tuttul</a></li><li><blockquote>Zalmaqum was the name of a region of northern Mesopotamia around Harran and the upper <b>Balikh</b> river.</blockquote><a href="/state/zalmaqum">Zalmaqum</a></li></ul></section>
